# Discriminative compression pilot analysis v1

## Validity

The native loader returned 11,314 training and 7,532 test documents across all
20 classes after the frozen metadata removal.  Train-only TF-IDF produced
17,797 sparse features for the full baseline.  The task completed in 44.25 s
including first acquisition/load, with a 467,021,824-byte peak RSS, well below
the 4 GiB process limit.  The raw cache remains under `.deps`.

## Results against the predeclared joint gate

- Full sparse Ridge: macro-F1 0.675548, accuracy 0.687732, method wall 2.57494 s.
- 25% uniform reservoir: macro-F1 0.599620--0.604034, losses
  0.071513--0.075927, and wall ratios 0.351--0.443.  All three seeds fail the
  quality gate despite substantial time savings.
- 50% uniform reservoir: macro-F1 0.640805--0.648674, losses
  0.026873--0.034743, and wall ratios 0.546--0.572.  One of three seeds passes
  both gates; two fail quality.  The median loss is 0.032212, just outside the
  0.03 threshold.  Selecting seed 211 after seeing test labels would be invalid.
- SVD-128: macro-F1 loss 0.077935 and wall ratio 1.323.
- SVD-256: macro-F1 loss 0.053282 and wall ratio 2.093.
  Both standard low-rank points are worse in quality and time than full sparse.

## Interpretation

The standard low-rank feature-compression baseline is a scoped negative on this
task.  A 25% example coreset is also clearly too aggressive.  The 50% uniform
reservoir result is a boundary signal rather than a confirmed benefit: a single
seed passes, but the component is not seed-robust under the observed three
points.  This pilot does not estimate a nominal failure probability and does
not validate a new coreset.

The remaining important uncertainty is whether the boundary behavior is merely
class-count variability and single-run timing noise.  One prospective follow-up
will compare five new uniform seeds with a stronger label-stratified 50%
sampling baseline and repeat the deterministic full baseline.  It will use a
fixed aggregate rule and then close or retain only this sampling direction.

